KEY OBSERVATION: on the SAME running V1 (2.53) remoting TCP server, the DIRECT PcbSeed is BLOCKED (the
outer ObjectInputStreamEx.resolveClass rejects the blacklisted class name) while the SAME PcbSeed WRAPPED
in a java.security.SignedObject REACHES (the outer stream only names SignedObject, not blacklisted;
SignedObject.getObject() reconstructs PcbSeed via a nested, unfiltered ObjectInputStream) -- demonstrated
over a REAL EXTERNAL TCP trigger. remoting-3.16 closes it by blacklisting java.security.SignedObject.

DATA-QUALITY: distinct remoting jars for THIS differential (2.52 -> 2.53 -> 3.16) with distinct ClassFilter
states (absent -> present-without-SignedObject -> present-with-SignedObject); original != residual on V1
(direct BLOCKED vs SignedObject-wrapped REACHES); V1 does NOT block the residual; V2 blocks both; V1
genuinely carries the CVE-2015-8103 guard (the direct seed IS rejected, PcbExpected control passes) and is
genuinely bypassed by the SignedObject residual it does not cover; SignedObject was NOT already blacklisted
in V1 (verified). BENIGN: PcbSeed.readObject only echoes the per-run ${PCB_FLAG}; transient flag never on
the wire; no gadget chain, no JNDI/RMI, no command execution.

